{:edb/db-before-t 878 :edb/db-after-t 879 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534191 50 #inst "2026-10-06T09:19:03.613Z" 13194139534191 true] [17592186100638 1100 "Consider the following differential equation.\n\n$$\ny' = -\\dfrac{y}{x}, \\qquad x \\neq 0\n$$\n\n \n\nLet $f(x) = \\dfrac{1}{x}.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $f'(x) = -\\dfrac{1}{x^2}$\n\n- Substituting $y = f(x)$ and $y' = f'(x)$ into the differential equation gives a true statement\n\n- The function $y=f(x)$ is a solution of the differential equation" 13194139534191 true] [17592186100638 1100 "Consider the following differential equation.\n$y' = -\\dfrac{y}{x}, \\qquad x \\neq 0$\n\nLet $f(x) = \\dfrac{1}{x}$. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $f'(x) = -\\dfrac{1}{x^2}$\n2. Substituting $y = f(x)$ and $y' = f'(x)$ into the differential equation gives a true statement\n3. The function $y=f(x)$ is a solution of the differential equation" 13194139534191 false] [17592186100638 1145 "For $x\\ne0$, differentiate $f(x)=x^{-1}$:\n$$f'(x)=-x^{-2}=-\\frac1{x^2}.$$\nThus statement I is true. Substitution into the right-hand side gives\n$$-\\frac{f(x)}x=-\\frac{1/x}x=-\\frac1{x^2}=f'(x),$$\nso II is true. This identity holds throughout either interval $x>0$ or $x<0$, making $f$ a solution and III true. The answer is **I, II, and III**." 13194139534191 false] [17592186100638 1145 "For a function $f(x)$ to be a solution to a differential equation, it must satisfy the equation for all values of $x$ in the equation's domain.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Differentiating $f(x) = \\dfrac{1}{x},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & ={(\\frac{1}{x})}^{′} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Substituting $y = \\dfrac{1}{x}$ and $y' = -\\dfrac{1}{x^2}$ into the original equation, we get a true statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & \\overset{?}{=}-\\frac{y}{x} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}} & \\overset{?}{=}-\\frac{(\\frac{1}{x})}{x} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}} & =-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statement II is true, the function $y = \\dfrac{1}{x}$ is a solution to our differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" 13194139534191 true] [17592186100708 1100 "Consider the following initial value problem\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = \\dfrac{3y}{x}, \\qquad y(-1) = -2.\n$$\n\nYou're given that $y = Ax^3$ is the solution to this initial value problem. What is the value of the constant $A?$" 13194139534191 true] [17592186100708 1100 "Consider the following initial value problem\n$\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = \\dfrac{3y}{x}, \\qquad y(-1) = -2$.\n\nYou're given that $y = Ax^3$ is the solution to this initial value problem. What is the value of the constant $A$?" 13194139534191 false] [17592186100708 1145 "We're given that $y = Ax^3$ is the solution to the initial value problem. Therefore, it must satisfy the differential equation *and* the initial condition $y(-1) = -2$.\n\nSubstituting the initial condition\n\n$$\ny({\\color{red}{-1}}) = {\\color{blue}{-2}}\n$$\n\ninto our solution and solving for $A$, we get\n\n$$\\begin{array}{rl}y & = A {x}^{3} \\\\ \\textcolor{blue}{- 2} & = A ( \\textcolor{red}{- 1} {)}^{3} \\\\ - 2 & = A ( - 1 ) \\\\ A & = 2.\\end{array}$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n$y = 2x^3$." 13194139534191 false] [17592186100708 1145 "We're given that $y = Ax^3$ is the solution to the initial value problem. Therefore, it must satisfy the differential equation *and* the initial condition $y(-1) = -2.$\n\nSubstituting the initial condition $y({\\color{red}{-1}}) = {\\color{blue}{-2}}$ into our solution and solving for $A,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =A{x}^{3} \\\\ -2 & =A(-1)^{3} \\\\ -2 & =A(-1) \\\\ A & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny = 2x^3 .\n$$" 13194139534191 true] [17592186100714 1100 "Consider the differential equation\n$\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = y$.\n\nWhich of the following is a solution to this equation?\n\n1. $y = e^x + 1$\n2. $y = -e^x$\n3. $y = 2e^x$" 13194139534191 false] [17592186100714 1100 "Consider the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = y.\n$$\n\n Which of the following is a solution to this equation?\n\n- $y = e^x + 1$\n\n- $y = -e^x$\n\n- $y = 2e^x$" 13194139534191 true] [17592186100714 1145 "A solution must satisfy $y'=y$ at every point of its interval. Differentiate each candidate and compare.\n\n- I: If $y=e^x+1$, then $y'=e^x\\ne e^x+1=y$. Therefore $y=e^x+1$ is not a solution.\n- II: If $y=-e^x$, then $y'=-e^x=y$. It is a solution.\n- III: If $y=2e^x$, then $y'=2e^x=y$. It is a solution.\n\nThe correct answer is **II and III only**." 13194139534191 false] [17592186100714 1145 "For a function $f(x)$ to be a solution to a differential equation, it must satisfy the equation for all values of $x$ in the equation's domain.\n\nWe check each proposed solution by differentiating and substituting it into the original equation. \n\n- Differentiating $y = e^x + 1,$ we get $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = e^x.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =y \\\\ {e}^{x} & \\overset{?}{=}{e}^{x}+1 \\\\ 0 & \\ne 1.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *false*. Therefore, the function $y = e^x+1$ is *not* a solution to our differential equation.\n\n- Differentiating $y = -e^x,$ we get $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = -e^x.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =y \\\\ -{e}^{x} & =-{e}^{x}.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *true*. Therefore, the function $y = -e^x$ *is* a solution to our differential equation.\n\n- Differentiating $y = 2e^x,$ we get $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = 2e^x.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =y \\\\ 2{e}^{x} & =2{e}^{x}.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *true*. Therefore, the function $y = 2e^x$ *is* a solution to our differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" 13194139534191 true] [17592186100801 1020 #edb/ref 17592186281384 13194139534191 true] [17592186100801 1020 #edb/ref 17592186281391 13194139534191 true] [17592186100801 1020 #edb/ref 17592186281398 13194139534191 true] [17592186100801 1020 #edb/ref 17592186281405 13194139534191 true] [17592186100868 1020 #edb/ref 17592186281440 13194139534191 true] [17592186100868 1020 #edb/ref 17592186281447 13194139534191 true] [17592186100868 1020 #edb/ref 17592186281450 13194139534191 true] [17592186100868 1020 #edb/ref 17592186281453 13194139534191 true] [17592186100868 1020 #edb/ref 17592186281456 13194139534191 true] [17592186100876 1020 #edb/ref 17592186281412 13194139534191 true] [17592186100876 1020 #edb/ref 17592186281419 13194139534191 true] [17592186100876 1020 #edb/ref 17592186281426 13194139534191 true] [17592186100876 1020 #edb/ref 17592186281433 13194139534191 true] [17592186101070 1100 "Consider the following differential equation.\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=2y\n$$\n\n \n\nLet $f(x)={e}^{2x}.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${f}^{′}(x)=2{e}^{2x}$\n- Substituting $y=f(x)$ and ${y}^{′}={f}^{′}(x)$ into the differential equation gives a true statement\n- The function $y=f(x)$ is a solution of the differential equation" 13194139534191 true] [17592186101070 1100 "Consider the following differential equation.\n$y^{\\prime } = 2y$\n\nLet $f(x) = e^{2x}$. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $f^{\\prime }(x) = 2e^{2x}$\n2. Substituting $y = f(x)$ and $y^{\\prime } = f^{\\prime }(x)$ into the differential equation gives a true statement\n3. The function $y = f(x)$ is a solution of the differential equation" 13194139534191 false] [17592186101070 1145 "For a function $f(x)$ to be a solution to a differential equation, it must satisfy the equation for all values of $x$ in the equation's domain.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Differentiating $f(x)={e}^{2x},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & ={({e}^{2x})}^{′} \\\\  & =2{e}^{2x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Substituting $y={e}^{2x}$ and ${y}^{′}=2{e}^{2x}$ into the original equation, we get a true statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & \\overset{?}{=}2y \\\\ 2{e}^{2x} & =2{e}^{2x}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statement II is true, the function $y={e}^{2x}$ is a solution to our differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" 13194139534191 true] [17592186101070 1145 "The chain rule gives $f'(x)=2e^{2x}$, so I is true. The right-hand side is $2f(x)=2e^{2x}=f'(x)$ for all $x$, so II is true and $f$ is a solution, making III true. Select **I, II, and III**." 13194139534191 false] [17592186101451 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534191 false] [17592186101451 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534191 true] [17592186101451 1100 "Consider the differential equation\n\n$\\frac{dy}{dx} = \\frac{2y}{x} - 2, x \\ne 0$.\n\nWhich of the following is a solution to this equation?\n\n1. $y = 2x$\n2. $y = x^{2}$\n3. $y = x^{3}$" 13194139534191 false] [17592186101451 1100 "Consider the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{2y}{x}-2,\\,x\\ne 0.\n$$\n\n Which of the following is a solution to this equation?\n\n- $y=2x$\n- $y={x}^{2}$\n- $y={x}^{3}$" 13194139534191 true] [17592186101451 1145 "For $x\\ne0$, compare each derivative with $2y/x-2$.\n\n- I: for $y=2x$, $y'=2$ and $2y/x-2=4-2=2$, so it works.\n- II: for $y=x^2$, $y'=2x$ but $2y/x-2=2x-2$, so it fails.\n- III: for $y=x^3$, $y'=3x^2$ but $2y/x-2=2x^2-2$, so it fails.\n\nSelect **I only**." 13194139534191 false] [17592186101451 1145 "For a function $f(x)$ to be a solution to a differential equation, it must satisfy the equation for all values of $x$ in the equation's domain.\n\nWe check each proposed solution by differentiating and substituting it into the original equation. \n\n- Differentiating $y=2x,$ we get $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=2.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{2y}{x}-2 \\\\ 2 & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{2\\cdot 2x}{x}-2 \\\\ 2 & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{4x}{x}-2 \\\\ 2 & \\overset{?}{=}4-2 \\\\ 2 & =2.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *true*. Therefore, the function $y=2x$ *is* a solution to our differential equation.\n\n- Differentiating $y={x}^{2},$ we get $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=2x.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{2y}{x}-2 \\\\ 2x & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{2\\cdot {x}^{2}}{x}-2 \\\\ 2x & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{x}-2 \\\\ 2x & \\overset{?}{=}2x-2 \\\\ 0 & \\ne -2.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *false*. Therefore, the function $y={x}^{2}$ is *not* a solution to our differential equation.\n\n- Differentiating $y={x}^{3},$ we get $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=3{x}^{2}.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{2y}{x}-2 \\\\ 3{x}^{2} & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{2\\cdot {x}^{3}}{x}-2 \\\\ 3{x}^{2} & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{2{x}^{3}}{x}-2 \\\\ 3{x}^{2} & \\overset{?}{=}2{x}^{2}-2 \\\\ {x}^{2} & \\ne -2\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *not true* for all $x.$ Therefore, the function $y={x}^{3}$ is *not* a solution to our differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139534191 true] [17592186281384 1017 #edb/ref 17592186281385 13194139534191 true] [17592186281384 1089 "q-141081" 13194139534191 true] [17592186281384 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534191 true] [17592186281384 1100 "Consider the following differential equation.\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=y\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x\n$$\n\n \n\nLet $f(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${f}^{′}(x)=-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x$\n- Substituting $y=f(x)$ and ${y}^{′}={f}^{′}(x)$ into the differential equation gives a true statement\n- The function $y=f(x)$ is a solution of the differential equation" 13194139534191 true] [17592186281384 1113 #uuid "ed1c0185-bf53-5e0e-8f4f-d5c9ea9f7c86" 13194139534191 true] [17592186281384 1145 "For a function $f(x)$ to be a solution to a differential equation, it must satisfy the equation for all values of $x$ in the equation's domain.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Differentiating $f(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & ={(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x)}^{′} \\\\  & =-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Substituting $y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x$ and ${y}^{′}=-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x$ into the original equation, we get a false statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & \\overset{?}{=}y\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x \\\\ -2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x & \\overset{?}{=}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡2x \\\\ -2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x & \\ne {cos}^{2}⁡2x\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since statement II is false, the function $y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x$ is not a solution to our differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139534191 true] [17592186281385 1000 #edb/ref 17592186281386 13194139534191 true] [17592186281385 1000 #edb/ref 17592186281387 13194139534191 true] [17592186281385 1000 #edb/ref 17592186281388 13194139534191 true] [17592186281385 1000 #edb/ref 17592186281389 13194139534191 true] [17592186281385 1000 #edb/ref 17592186281390 13194139534191 true] [17592186281385 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534191 true] [17592186281385 1041 "selection" 13194139534191 true] [17592186281385 1104 #edb/ref 17592186281388 13194139534191 true] [17592186281385 1110 #uuid "727d3302-be7f-55b9-87be-dc2fd7e7331d" 13194139534191 true] [17592186281386 1034 "I, II, and III" 13194139534191 true] [17592186281386 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534191 true] [17592186281386 1111 #uuid "414b32a6-1f12-5749-a310-b2a4bae616dd" 13194139534191 true] [17592186281387 1034 "II and III only" 13194139534191 true] [17592186281387 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534191 true] [17592186281387 1111 #uuid "6d65cf3f-748a-5527-9dc5-7b5ea788c014" 13194139534191 true] [17592186281388 1034 "I only" 13194139534191 true] [17592186281388 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534191 true] [17592186281388 1111 #uuid "17c7effc-7e27-5184-8825-b4c6d7d39a18" 13194139534191 true] [17592186281389 1034 "III only" 13194139534191 true] [17592186281389 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534191 true] [17592186281389 1111 #uuid "cd36af1f-6913-5e4a-9a85-c5c91ea87e4c" 13194139534191 true] [17592186281390 1034 "II only" 13194139534191 true] [17592186281390 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534191 true] [17592186281390 1111 #uuid "ded6d3f3-ed85-50ce-b37c-23927cf96273" 13194139534191 true] [17592186281391 1017 #edb/ref 17592186281392 13194139534191 true] [17592186281391 1089 "q-141084" 13194139534191 true] [17592186281391 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534191 true] [17592186281391 1100 "Consider the following differential equation.\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}},\\,x\\ne 0\n$$\n\n \n\nLet $f(x)=-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${f}^{′}(x)=-\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}}$\n- Substituting $y=f(x)$ and ${y}^{′}={f}^{′}(x)$ into the differential equation gives a true statement\n- The function $y=f(x)$ is a solution of the differential equation" 13194139534191 true] [17592186281391 1113 #uuid "dd7f154e-bda2-5ad0-9a82-1b52f8f224b6" 13194139534191 true] [17592186281391 1145 "For a function $f(x)$ to be a solution to a differential equation, it must satisfy the equation for all values of $x$ in the equation's domain.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Differentiating $f(x)=-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & ={(-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})}^{′} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}} \\\\  & \\ne -\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Substituting $y=-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}$ and ${y}^{′}=\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}}$ into the original equation, we get a false statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{{x}^{3}} & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{(-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})}{{x}^{2}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{{x}^{3}} & \\ne -\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}}\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since statement II is false, the function $y=-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}$ is not a solution to our differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None.\"" 13194139534191 true] [17592186281392 1000 #edb/ref 17592186281393 13194139534191 true] [17592186281392 1000 #edb/ref 17592186281394 13194139534191 true] [17592186281392 1000 #edb/ref 17592186281395 13194139534191 true] [17592186281392 1000 #edb/ref 17592186281396 13194139534191 true] [17592186281392 1000 #edb/ref 17592186281397 13194139534191 true] [17592186281392 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534191 true] [17592186281392 1041 "selection" 13194139534191 true] [17592186281392 1104 #edb/ref 17592186281397 13194139534191 true] [17592186281392 1110 #uuid "28919156-568a-5af2-bd1e-f2edbd4e929c" 13194139534191 true] [17592186281393 1034 "I, II, and III" 13194139534191 true] [17592186281393 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534191 true] [17592186281393 1111 #uuid "90bc8aa8-bab3-5613-9eac-d17417da671d" 13194139534191 true] [17592186281394 1034 "II and III only" 13194139534191 true] [17592186281394 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534191 true] [17592186281394 1111 #uuid "9f50fb7e-7563-5542-8980-5976cd65691d" 13194139534191 true] [17592186281395 1034 "I only" 13194139534191 true] [17592186281395 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534191 true] [17592186281395 1111 #uuid "f06491ec-ec91-52e1-a937-106058d39f8b" 13194139534191 true] [17592186281396 1034 "III only" 13194139534191 true] [17592186281396 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534191 true] [17592186281396 1111 #uuid "e07d4240-42d1-58a5-90cd-5ebfeb0eec55" 13194139534191 true] [17592186281397 1034 "None" 13194139534191 true] [17592186281397 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534191 true] [17592186281397 1111 #uuid "7db58664-e7cc-5cc9-a75a-f8e43152cbbf" 13194139534191 true] [17592186281398 1017 #edb/ref 17592186281399 13194139534191 true] [17592186281398 1089 "q-141088" 13194139534191 true] [17592186281398 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534191 true] [17592186281398 1100 "Consider the following differential equation.\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\frac{4x}{{e}^{y/2}}\n$$\n\n \n\nLet $f(x)=4\\operatorname{ln}⁡x.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${f}^{′}(x)=\\frac{1}{4x}$\n- Substituting $y=f(x)$ and ${y}^{′}={f}^{′}(x)$ into the differential equation gives a true statement\n- The function $y=f(x)$ is a solution of the differential equation" 13194139534191 true] [17592186281398 1113 #uuid "d4fe977c-8459-57c9-ab17-2b9db9bf0419" 13194139534191 true] [17592186281398 1145 "For a function $f(x)$ to be a solution to a differential equation, it must satisfy the equation for all values of $x$ in the equation's domain.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Differentiating $f(x)=4\\operatorname{ln}⁡x,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & ={(4\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)}^{′} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{x} \\\\  & \\ne \\frac{1}{4x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Substituting $y=4\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ and ${y}^{′}=\\frac{4}{x}$ into the original equation, we get a true statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{4x}{{e}^{y/2}} \\\\ \\frac{4}{x} & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{4x}{{e}^{(4\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)/2}} \\\\ \\frac{4}{x} & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{4x}{{e}^{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}} \\\\ \\frac{4}{x} & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{4x}{{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡{x}^{2}}} \\\\ \\frac{4}{x} & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{4x}{{x}^{2}} \\\\ \\frac{4}{x} & =\\frac{4}{x}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statement II is true, the function $y=4\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ is a solution to our differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" 13194139534191 true] [17592186281399 1000 #edb/ref 17592186281400 13194139534191 true] [17592186281399 1000 #edb/ref 17592186281401 13194139534191 true] [17592186281399 1000 #edb/ref 17592186281402 13194139534191 true] [17592186281399 1000 #edb/ref 17592186281403 13194139534191 true] [17592186281399 1000 #edb/ref 17592186281404 13194139534191 true] [17592186281399 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534191 true] [17592186281399 1041 "selection" 13194139534191 true] [17592186281399 1104 #edb/ref 17592186281401 13194139534191 true] [17592186281399 1110 #uuid "ae68cede-a41f-5118-995f-4470e2d96cac" 13194139534191 true] [17592186281400 1034 "I, II, and III" 13194139534191 true] [17592186281400 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534191 true] [17592186281400 1111 #uuid "04d0d786-724e-5978-9e04-6236d7ec120c" 13194139534191 true] [17592186281401 1034 "II and III only" 13194139534191 true] [17592186281401 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534191 true] [17592186281401 1111 #uuid "e1cd3ddc-09b8-5d96-8914-8a10dc5394cb" 13194139534191 true] [17592186281402 1034 "I only" 13194139534191 true] [17592186281402 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534191 true] [17592186281402 1111 #uuid "d9d53274-b46a-5221-8c99-26771bb598ae" 13194139534191 true] [17592186281403 1034 "III only" 13194139534191 true] [17592186281403 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534191 true] [17592186281403 1111 #uuid "9ef6e210-c142-5d78-a9a2-495d45d9f947" 13194139534191 true] [17592186281404 1034 "None" 13194139534191 true] [17592186281404 1103 #edb/ref 175921860